  "Question": "The equation \\(\\sqrt{x+10}-\\dfrac6{\\sqrt{x+10}}=5\\) has:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "an extraneous root between \\(-5\\) and \\(-1\\)",
    "B": "an extraneous root between \\(-10\\) and\n\\(-6\\)",
    "C": "a true root between \\(20\\) and \\(25\\)",
    "D": "two true roots",
    "E": "two extraneous roots"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The square root must be real and the denominator must be nonzero, so \\(x>-10\\). On this domain, multiplying by \\(\\sqrt{x+10}\\) is valid and gives\n\\[\nx+10-6=5\\sqrt{x+10},\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nx+4=5\\sqrt{x+10}.\n\\]\nSquaring removes the radical, but it may introduce a candidate whose two sides had opposite signs before squaring. We obtain\n\\[\n(x+4)^2=25(x+10),\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to\n\\[\nx^2-17x-234=(x-26)(x+9)=0.\n\\]\nEvery original solution must therefore be \\(26\\) or \\(-9\\). Checking both in the original equation gives\n\\[\nx=26:\\quad 6-\\frac66=5,\n\\]\n\\[\nx=-9:\\quad 1-\\frac61=-5\\ne5.\n\\]\nThus \\(26\\) is the only true root, while \\(-9\\) is extraneous. Since \\(-10<-9<-6\\), the answer is choice B.",
